"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,391\nEpisode 5\n\n3\n00:00:07,471 --> 00:00:09,577\nBuilding on what\u2019s there\n\n4\n00:00:12,377 --> 00:00:13,513\nIn a classic study,\n\n5\n00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:17,180\npeople heard a passage written in deliberately vague terms.\n\n6\n00:00:17,479 --> 00:00:19,170\nSort the items into groups.\n\n7\n00:00:19,407 --> 00:00:21,039\nOne pile may be enough.\n\n8\n00:00:21,388 --> 00:00:22,771\nThe procedure is simple,\n\n9\n00:00:22,791 --> 00:00:24,533\nbut mistakes can be costly.\n\n10\n00:00:24,889 --> 00:00:28,634\nMost listeners found it hard to follow,\n\n11\n00:00:29,014 --> 00:00:30,600\nand harder to remember.\n\n12\n00:00:30,897 --> 00:00:32,339\nThen some were told the topic: washing clothes.\n\n13\n00:00:32,509 --> 00:00:33,525\nTiming mattered.\n\n14\n00:00:33,943 --> 00:00:36,916\nPeople told the topic before the passage understood it,\n\n15\n00:00:37,054 --> 00:00:38,532\nand remembered far more.\n\n16\n00:00:38,944 --> 00:00:42,453\nPeople told afterwards did no better than those never told.\n\n17\n00:00:42,844 --> 00:00:44,358\nIn a version with a picture,\n\n18\n00:00:44,587 --> 00:00:48,770\npeople who saw it first recalled about eight ideas out of fourteen.\n\n19\n00:00:49,193 --> 00:00:52,106\nPeople who saw it afterwards recalled under four.\n\n20\n00:00:52,529 --> 00:00:55,718\nKnowledge helps only if it is active while you learn.\n\n21\n00:00:55,888 --> 00:00:57,751\nExperts show the same pattern.\n\n22\n00:00:58,189 --> 00:01:01,506\nAfter five seconds looking at a board from a real game,\n\n23\n00:01:01,673 --> 00:01:04,633\nchess masters could rebuild it almost perfectly.\n\n24\n00:01:05,076 --> 00:01:07,517\nNovices placed only a few pieces.\n\n25\n00:01:07,828 --> 00:01:10,028\nBut when the pieces were scattered at random,\n\n26\n00:01:10,204 --> 00:01:12,619\nthe masters' advantage nearly vanished.\n\n27\n00:01:13,047 --> 00:01:15,335\nTheir memory was not better in general.\n\n28\n00:01:15,638 --> 00:01:18,212\nIt was built on patterns they already knew.\n\n29\n00:01:18,382 --> 00:01:20,996\nSchemas even speed up consolidation.\n\n30\n00:01:21,514 --> 00:01:25,812\nRats spent many sessions learning where six flavors were hidden in an arena.\n\n31\n00:01:26,281 --> 00:01:27,852\nOnce that map was in place,\n\n32\n00:01:28,077 --> 00:01:31,197\nthey learned a new flavor location in a single trial,\n\n33\n00:01:31,469 --> 00:01:35,265\nand within two days the memory no longer needed the hippocampus.\n\n34\n00:01:35,762 --> 00:01:40,454\nRats trained in an arena where the layout kept changing could not do this.\n\n35\n00:01:40,624 --> 00:01:41,427\nA caution.\n\n36\n00:01:41,821 --> 00:01:44,041\nPrior knowledge helps when it is accurate.\n\n37\n00:01:44,453 --> 00:01:45,397\nWhen it is wrong,\n\n38\n00:01:45,419 --> 00:01:48,286\nnew material gets bent to fit the old schema,\n\n39\n00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:51,227\nand misconceptions can survive instruction.\n\n40\n00:01:51,397 --> 00:01:52,180\nFor teaching.\n\n41\n00:01:52,566 --> 00:01:55,882\nBefore new material, activate what students already know: a question,\n\n42\n00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:59,868\na familiar example, the big picture first.\n\n43\n00:02:00,326 --> 00:02:02,922\nGive the topic and structure before the details,\n\n44\n00:02:02,942 --> 00:02:03,645\nnot after.\n\n45\n00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:06,112\nCheck prior knowledge for errors,\n\n46\n00:02:06,132 --> 00:02:07,196\nnot just gaps.\n\n47\n00:02:07,465 --> 00:02:11,814\nAnd build courses so early ideas become the frame for later ones.\n\n48\n00:02:11,984 --> 00:02:14,406\nNew learning needs something to hold on to.\n\n49\n00:02:14,710 --> 00:02:16,177\nBuild the frame first.\n\n"
